//? Title: Almeida-Pineda: Backpropagation Through a Fixed Point
//? Subtitle: Recurrent networks that settle, the adjoint equation from the implicit function theorem, and an iris classifier with datamunge's stats::AlmeidaPinedaClassifier
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A recurrent network whose units feed back into each other computes by
*settling*: its output is a fixed point \(x=\tanh(Wx)\), not the end of a
forward sweep. Almeida and Pineda showed in 1987 how to train such a network
without unrolling it in time. Settle the network forward. Then settle a
second, linear *adjoint* system that runs on the transposed weights. The
gradient is the outer product of the two equilibria. We derive the adjoint
equation from the implicit function theorem and prove that the adjoint
converges at exactly the forward relaxation's rate. Both claims are checked
numerically: the gradient matches central finite differences to
\(3\times10^{-10}\), and both residual curves contract by the same factor,
0.674 per sweep. A fully recurrent network with five hidden units, trained
on the iris data from `dm::datasets::iris()`, misclassifies 1 of 45 test
flowers, as LDA does. It settles in about 40 sweeps per flower. Of 31 500 training
gradients, 18 (0.06%) had a relaxation that failed to converge, in short
bursts while the weights moved fastest.
)

\toc

> Networks that settle

Take \(N\) units. The first \(m\) are *clamped* to the input: a constant
bias unit and the predictors. The others are *free*. Every free unit \(i\)
receives every other unit through a weight \(W_{ij}\), so hidden and output
units feed back into each other. The network's response to an input is a
fixed point of

$$
x_i = f(a_i),\qquad a_i=\sum_{j} W_{ij}x_j \qquad (i>m),\qquad f=\tanh .
$$
\alttext(x sub i equals f of a sub i, where a sub i is the sum over j of W i j times x j, for each free unit i, and f is tanh.)

**Existence.** \(\tanh\) maps into \([-1,1]\), so the map
\(x\mapsto\tanh(Wx)\) sends the cube \([-1,1]^{N-m}\) into itself. By
Brouwer's theorem it has a fixed point.

**Uniqueness and convergence.** Suppose
\(\|W_{ff}\|\cdot\max f'<1\), where \(W_{ff}\) is the free-to-free block.
Then the map is a contraction, so the fixed point is unique and the
iteration converges to it from anywhere, by Banach's theorem. Since
\(f'\le1\), a sufficient condition is \(\|W_{ff}\|<1\). Training does not
enforce this, but the networks below still converge, and the library
reports any relaxation that fails to.

The relaxation uses synchronous, damped sweeps,
\(x\leftarrow(1-\rho)x+\rho\tanh(Wx)\) with \(\rho=\tfrac12\). Near the
fixed point the error contracts each sweep by the spectral radius of
\((1-\rho)I+\rho DW_{ff}\), where \(D=\operatorname{diag}f'(a)\).

> The gradient from the implicit function theorem

Let the output units carry targets \(t_k\), with error
\(E=\tfrac12\sum_k(x_k-t_k)^2\) and \(e_i=x_i-t_i\) on output units (zero
elsewhere). The fixed point satisfies \(F(x,W)=x-f(Wx)=0\).
Differentiating gives

$$
dx = D\,(dW)\,x + D\,W\,dx
\;\;\Longrightarrow\;\;
dx=(I-DW)^{-1}D\,(dW)\,x ,
$$
\alttext(dx equals D times dW times x plus D W dx, so dx equals the inverse of I minus D W, times D, dW, x.)

provided \(I-DW\) is invertible, which holds when the fixed point is
stable. Then \(dE=e^\top dx\). Define the adjoint vector \(y\) by
\(y^\top=e^\top(I-DW)^{-1}D\). Then \(dE=y^\top(dW)\,x\), so

$$
\frac{\partial E}{\partial W_{ij}} = y_i\,x_j ,\qquad
y = D\,\bigl(W^\top y + e\bigr).
$$
\alttext(The partial derivative of E with respect to W i j equals y i times x j, where y equals D times W transpose y plus e.)

To check the second equation, let \(z=D^{-1}y\). The definition gives
\(z^\top(I-DW)=e^\top\), so \(z=W^\top Dz+e=W^\top y+e\). The adjoint is
itself a fixed-point equation. It is linear, and it can be solved by the
same kind of relaxation, run backwards through the transposed weights,
with no stored history. That is the whole algorithm: settle \(x\), settle
\(y\), take the outer product.

**Theorem (equal rates).** The undamped adjoint iteration
\(y\leftarrow D(W^\top y+e)\) has iteration matrix \(DW^\top\). Now

$$
DW^\top = D\,(W^\top D)\,D^{-1},
$$
\alttext(D W transpose equals D times W transpose D times D inverse.)

so \(DW^\top\) is similar to \(W^\top D=(DW)^\top\), whose eigenvalues are
those of the forward Jacobian \(DW\). With damping, both matrices take the
form \((1-\rho)I+\rho M\). So if the forward relaxation is locally stable,
the adjoint converges too, and at the same asymptotic rate. This is
Almeida's stability result. \(\square\)

`algorithms::almeida_pineda_settle` and `almeida_pineda_gradient` expose
both relaxations and their residuals. `stats::AlmeidaPinedaClassifier`
wraps them as a classifier fitted from a formula and a DataFrame.

> Checking the gradient and the rates

The test network has 3 clamped units, 5 free units and 2 outputs, with
random weights. The table compares the Almeida-Pineda gradient with central
differences \((E(W+h)-E(W-h))/2h\), \(h=10^{-6}\), for every weight. The
figure plots the largest change per sweep in the forward and adjoint
relaxations.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/almeida_pineda_residuals.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto net = alg::almeida_pineda_network(8, 3, {6, 7}, 0.5, 2026);
  const std::vector<double> input{1.0, 0.7, -1.2}, target{0.6, -0.4};
  const alg::AlmeidaPinedaSettleOptions options{5000, 1e-14, 0.5};
  const auto g = alg::almeida_pineda_gradient(net, input, target, options);

  double worst = 0.0, largest = 0.0;
  int checked = 0;
  for (std::size_t i = 3; i < 8; ++i)
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < 8; ++j) {
      if (i == j) continue;
      auto plus = net, minus = net;
      plus.W[i][j] += 1e-6;
      minus.W[i][j] -= 1e-6;
      const double fd = (alg::almeida_pineda_gradient(plus, input, target, options).error -
                         alg::almeida_pineda_gradient(minus, input, target, options).error) / 2e-6;
      worst = std::max(worst, std::abs(fd - g.dW[i][j]));
      largest = std::max(largest, std::abs(g.dW[i][j]));
      ++checked;
    }
  std::print("| quantity | value |\n| :--- | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| error E | {:.6f} |\n", g.error);
  std::print("| weights checked | {} |\n", checked);
  std::print("| largest gradient entry | {:.4e} |\n", largest);
  std::print("| largest difference from finite differences | {:.2e} |\n", worst);
  std::print("| forward sweeps to 1e-14 | {} |\n", g.forward.iterations);
  std::print("| adjoint sweeps to 1e-14 | {} |\n", g.adjoint_iterations);
  // Mean contraction per sweep over sweeps 15..75 (the per-sweep ratio oscillates; see below).
  auto rate = [](const std::vector<double>& r) { return std::pow(r[75] / r[15], 1.0 / 60); };
  std::print("| forward contraction per sweep, sweeps 15-75 | {:.4f} |\n", rate(g.forward.residuals));
  std::print("| adjoint contraction per sweep, sweeps 15-75 | {:.4f} |\n", rate(g.adjoint_residuals));

  std::vector<double> kf, rf, ka, ra;
  for (std::size_t k = 0; k < g.forward.residuals.size(); ++k)
    if (g.forward.residuals[k] > 0) kf.push_back(k + 1.0), rf.push_back(std::log10(g.forward.residuals[k]));
  for (std::size_t k = 0; k < g.adjoint_residuals.size(); ++k)
    if (g.adjoint_residuals[k] > 0) ka.push_back(k + 1.0), ra.push_back(std::log10(g.adjoint_residuals[k]));
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(kf, rf, "forward x", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0)
      .lines(ka, ra, "adjoint y", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2.0)
      .title("Forward and adjoint relaxations")
      .x_label("sweep")
      .y_label("log10 largest change")
      .size(860, 480)
      .save_svg("build/almeida_pineda_residuals.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| quantity | value |
| :--- | ---: |
| error E | 1.173263 |
| weights checked | 35 |
| largest gradient entry | 1.2145e+00 |
| largest difference from finite differences | 2.98e-10 |
| forward sweeps to 1e-14 | 83 |
| adjoint sweeps to 1e-14 | 81 |
| forward contraction per sweep, sweeps 15-75 | 0.6738 |
| adjoint contraction per sweep, sweeps 15-75 | 0.6737 |
\image(figures/almeida_pineda_residuals.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The Almeida-Pineda gradient against central finite differences for every weight of a random network, and the convergence of the two relaxations)
\alttext(Two lines of log10 residual against sweep number, both falling from about 0 to minus 14 over about 80 sweeps. Both have the same overall slope and a matching dip every 30 sweeps or so; the red adjoint line runs parallel to the blue forward line.)

All 35 weights agree with finite differences to \(3\times10^{-10}\). Central
differences with \(h=10^{-6}\) have truncation error of order \(h^2\) and
rounding error of order \(\epsilon/h\), so that is the expected accuracy.
The gradient is exact up to the relaxation tolerance.

Both residual curves fall with the same overall slope, as the theorem
requires. Both also dip together about every 30 sweeps, because the
dominant eigenvalues of \(DW\) are a complex pair:
the per-sweep ratio oscillates around the modulus. Averaged over 60 sweeps,
both relaxations contract by the same factor, 0.674 per sweep.

> Classifying iris flowers

`dm::datasets::iris()` has 150 flowers, three species and four
measurements. The network has:

- 1 bias unit and 4 standardised inputs, all clamped;
- 5 hidden units and 3 output units, one per species, all free.

That makes 13 units and \(8\times12=96\) weights, since free units have
no self-loops. The targets are \(+0.8\) for the true species and \(-0.8\)
for the other two. Training runs 300 epochs of full-batch gradient descent
with momentum 0.9, on 105 flowers stratified by species. The other 45 are
held out.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/almeida_pineda_training.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto [train, test] = dm::stats::train_test_split(dm::datasets::iris(), 0.3, 1, "Species");
  const dm::stats::AlmeidaPinedaClassifier model(train, "Species ~ .");
  const dm::stats::LDA lda(train, "Species ~ .");
  const auto lda_pred = lda.predict(test);
  const auto& truth = test.get_column<std::string>("Species").values();
  double lda_wrong = 0;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < truth.size(); ++i) lda_wrong += lda_pred[i] != truth[i];

  const auto history = model.history();
  std::print("Training rows {}, test rows {}, weights {}\n\n", model.observations(), truth.size(),
             8 * 12);
  std::print("{}\n", history | dm::filter_rows([](const auto& row) {
                       const double e = row.get_double("epoch");
                       return e == 1 || e == 10 || e == 25 || e == 50 || e == 100 || e == 200 || e == 300;
                     }) | dm::to_md(4));
  std::print("| model | training error | test error |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| Almeida-Pineda network | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |\n", model.error(train), model.error(test));
  std::print("| LDA | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |\n\n", 1.0 - lda.training_accuracy(), lda_wrong / truth.size());
  double unconverged = 0;
  std::vector<int> epochs_hit;
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < history.nrows(); ++r)
    if (history.double_at("unconverged", r) > 0) {
      unconverged += history.double_at("unconverged", r);
      epochs_hit.push_back(static_cast<int>(history.double_at("epoch", r)));
    }
  std::print("Relaxations that hit the 500-sweep limit, over all {} x {} training gradients: {}, in epochs {}\n\n",
             history.nrows(), model.observations(), unconverged, epochs_hit);
  std::print("Test confusion matrix (rows: actual, columns: predicted):\n\n{}\n", model.confusion(test) | dm::to_md(0));

  const auto epoch = history.get_column<double>("epoch").values();
  std::vector<double> log_loss, iters;
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < history.nrows(); ++r) {
    log_loss.push_back(std::log10(history.double_at("loss", r)));
    iters.push_back(history.double_at("mean_iterations", r));
  }
  auto layout = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 3);
  layout.add(dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                 .lines(epoch, log_loss, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0)
                 .title("Training loss")
                 .x_label("epoch")
                 .y_label("log10 mean E"));
  layout.add(dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                 .lines(epoch, history.get_column<double>("train_error").values(), "", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2.0)
                 .y_limits(0, 0.7)
                 .title("Training error")
                 .x_label("epoch")
                 .y_label("misclassified"));
  layout.add(dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                 .lines(epoch, iters, "", dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 2.0)
                 .title("Sweeps to settle")
                 .x_label("epoch")
                 .y_label("mean forward sweeps"));
  layout.size(1200, 400).save_svg("build/almeida_pineda_training.svg");
}
```
