| :--- | ---: | ---: |
| Almeida-Pineda network | 0.010 | 0.022 |
| LDA | 0.010 | 0.022 |

Relaxations that hit the 500-sweep limit, over all 300 x 105 training gradients: 18, in epochs [47, 50, 52, 53, 54, 140, 145, 146, 156, 167, 168, 175, 181, 186, 234, 235]

Test confusion matrix (rows: actual, columns: predicted):

| actual | setosa | versicolor | virginica |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| setosa | 15 | 0 | 0 |
| versicolor | 0 | 14 | 1 |
| virginica | 0 | 0 | 15 |

\image(figures/almeida_pineda_training.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Training history at selected epochs, training and test error against LDA, and the test confusion matrix)
\alttext(Three panels against epoch. Left: log10 training loss falls steeply over the first 50 epochs to about minus 1.2, with spikes near epochs 55, 140, 185 and 235, and ends near minus 1.3. Middle: training error drops from 0.61 to about 0.02 within 25 epochs. Right: the mean number of forward sweeps per flower stays between about 35 and 60, rising around epochs 50 and 140 to 190.)

Training behaves like any small neural network:

- **Loss and error.** The loss falls twentyfold, from 1.02 to 0.05.
  Training error drops from 61% at the random start to 1 or 2 flowers
  within 25 epochs.
- **Test performance.** One test flower is misclassified: a versicolor
  called virginica, the boundary every linear method struggles with. LDA
  on the same split makes the same number of errors.
- **Settling.** The mean number of sweeps per flower stays between about
  35 and 60. Single flowers occasionally take much longer: 218 sweeps at
  epoch 300.
- **Occasional failures.** In 18 of the 31 500 training gradients, a
  relaxation hit the 500-sweep limit. They come in bursts, around epochs
  47 to 54, 140 to 186, and 234 to 235, where one or two flowers sit near
  a bifurcation of the dynamics. Each contributes a slightly wrong
  gradient for one flower in one epoch, and momentum averages these out.
  These are also the epochs where the loss jumps and the mean number of
  sweeps rises, in the left and right panels. A slowly converging
  relaxation is the visible sign of a fixed point close to losing
  stability. A deep-equilibrium implementation would switch to a root
  finder at such points.

> What the network settles to

The table shows the equilibrium of the three output units for a few
test flowers, with the number of sweeps each needed. The prediction is the
largest output.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  const auto [train, test] = dm::stats::train_test_split(dm::datasets::iris(), 0.3, 1, "Species");
  const dm::stats::AlmeidaPinedaClassifier model(train, "Species ~ .");
  auto out = model.outputs(test);
  out.add_column("Species", test.get_column<std::string>("Species").values());
  out.add_column("predicted", model.predict(test));
  const auto view = out | dm::select("Species", "predicted", "setosa", "versicolor", "virginica", "iterations");
  std::print("{}\n", view | dm::slice_head(8) | dm::to_md(3));
  const auto wrong = view | dm::filter_rows([](const auto& row) {
                       return row.get_string("Species") != row.get_string("predicted");
                     });
  std::print("Misclassified test flowers:\n\n{}\n", wrong | dm::to_md(3));
}
```
